::CW_U02_L01_Q01::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which sentence uses action as the main narrative technique?{
=A. I shoved the folded note into my jacket pocket before anyone saw it.#Correct. This sentence focuses on what the narrator does, so action is the main technique.
~B. The porch smelled like rain and old wood.#Incorrect. This is description because it uses sensory detail to create setting.
~C. "Do not open that," Reese whispered.#Incorrect. This is dialogue because it gives exact spoken words.
~D. I wondered whether the note had been meant for me.#Incorrect. This is reflection because it shows the narrator thinking.
}

::CW_U02_L01_Q02::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which choice best explains the purpose of a transition in narrative writing?{
~A. A transition always makes writing longer.#Incorrect. Transitions can be short or long. Their purpose is connection, not length.
~B. A transition replaces the need for action or description.#Incorrect. Transitions connect moments, but they do not replace narrative techniques.
=C. A transition helps the reader understand how one moment connects to another.#Correct. Transitions can show time, place, cause, contrast, sequence, or a shift in focus.
~D. A transition is only needed in essays, not stories.#Incorrect. Narratives also need transitions so readers can follow movement through time, place, and events.
}

::CW_U02_L01_Q03::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which revision best changes a plain event list into a developed scene? Event list: "The door opened. I saw my cousin. I smiled."{
~A. The door opened, I saw my cousin, and I smiled.#Incorrect. This keeps the events in a compressed list instead of developing them.
~B. First the door opened. Next I saw my cousin. Finally I smiled.#Incorrect. Mechanical transitions alone do not create a developed scene.
~C. I smiled because I saw my cousin after the door opened.#Incorrect. This explains the event but does not show it with narrative techniques.
=D. The door opened slowly, and my cousin stood there holding a backpack with both hands. For a second, neither of us moved. Then I smiled so hard my cheeks hurt.#Correct. This revision uses action, description, and a time transition to develop the moment.
}

::CW_U02_L01_Q04::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which sentence uses dialogue?{
~A. Nia tapped the glass twice and waited.#Incorrect. This is action. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. "Is anyone in there?" Nia asked.#Correct. Dialogue gives the exact words a character speaks.
~C. The glass was cold enough to fog beneath her fingers.#Incorrect. This is description. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Nia wondered if anyone would answer.#Incorrect. This is reflection. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_L01_Q05::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which transition best shows contrast?{
~A. After dinner, Malik washed the trophy.#Incorrect. This mainly shows time. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Because the trophy broke, Malik frowned.#Incorrect. This mainly shows cause. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Outside, the crowd cheered; inside, Malik stared at the broken trophy.#Correct. This transition contrasts the outside celebration with Malik's private disappointment.
~D. Beside the window, Malik set the trophy down.#Incorrect. This mainly shows place. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_L01_Q06::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] A student writes, "Then I walked home. Then I opened the door. Then I saw the lights." What is the clearest revision advice?{
=A. Replace some repeated "then" transitions with more specific time, place, or focus transitions.#Correct. More specific transitions help the reader understand movement without sounding mechanical.
~B. Keep repeating "then" because repetition always creates suspense.#Incorrect. Repetition can be useful, but here it is mechanical and does not clarify relationships.
~C. Remove all transitions so the reader has to guess the order.#Incorrect. The reader should not have to guess basic sequence.
~D. Add dialogue to every sentence.#Incorrect. Dialogue is useful only when spoken words matter to the scene.
}

::CW_U02_L01_Q07::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which choice is reflection?{
~A. The hallway narrowed near the music room.#Incorrect. This is description of place. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. "Wait for me," I called.#Incorrect. This is dialogue. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. I turned the corner and stopped.#Incorrect. This is action. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. I realized I had been hoping the letter would never arrive.#Correct. This sentence shows the narrator's thought and realization.
}

::CW_U02_L01_Q08::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which sentence uses description to develop mood?{
~A. I knocked three times.#Incorrect. This is action. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. The hallway light flickered over the peeling green paint.#Correct. The sensory details create a visual mood for the scene.
~C. "Come in," she said.#Incorrect. This is dialogue. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. I understood why she sounded afraid.#Incorrect. This is reflection. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_L01_Q09::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] What is the main problem with this draft? "First, the dog barked. Next, I ran. Finally, I hid behind the shed."{
~A. It has no sequence at all.#Incorrect. The sequence is clear because the events are in order.
~B. It uses too much dialogue.#Incorrect. The draft contains no dialogue. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. It relies on mechanical transitions instead of developing the moment with specific action, cause, or description.#Correct. The draft tells the sequence but does not create a vivid scene.
~D. It uses description instead of action.#Incorrect. It has action, but the action is undeveloped.
}

::CW_U02_L01_Q10::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which transition best shows time?{
~A. Under the bench, my ticket curled at the edge.#Incorrect. This shows place. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Because I had forgotten my ticket, I could not board.#Incorrect. This shows cause. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Even though I was nervous, I stepped forward.#Incorrect. This shows contrast or concession. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Before sunrise, the bus station was nearly empty.#Correct. "Before sunrise" tells when the moment happens.
}

::CW_U02_L01_Q11::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which choice best explains why dialogue should be used intentionally?{
=A. Dialogue should reveal character, conflict, relationship, or information that matters to the scene.#Correct. Dialogue is a narrative technique that should do real story work.
~B. Dialogue should appear in every sentence of a narrative.#Incorrect. Too much dialogue can weaken a scene if it is not purposeful.
~C. Dialogue should only repeat information the narrator already gave.#Incorrect. Strong dialogue should add conflict, voice, relationship, or information.
~D. Dialogue should replace transitions.#Incorrect. Dialogue and transitions do different jobs. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_L01_Q12::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which transition best shows cause and effect?{
~A. Across the room, the curtain moved.#Incorrect. This shows place. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Meanwhile, the hallway stayed quiet.#Incorrect. This shows simultaneous action. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Since the window was open, the papers slid off the desk.#Correct. "Since the window was open" explains the cause of the papers moving.
~D. Later, the papers were gone.#Incorrect. This shows time. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_L01_Q13::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which version most clearly uses action to show nervousness?{
~A. I was very nervous.#Incorrect. This tells the feeling directly instead of showing it through action.
=B. I twisted the bracelet around my wrist until the clasp pinched my skin.#Correct. The action reveals nervousness without directly naming it.
~C. Nervousness is uncomfortable.#Incorrect. This states a general idea, not a narrative moment.
~D. The room was blue and quiet.#Incorrect. This describes setting but does not clearly show nervousness.
}

::CW_U02_L01_Q14::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which choice is the best example of a focus transition?{
~A. Two hours later, the rain stopped.#Incorrect. This is a time transition. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Behind the gym, the pavement steamed.#Incorrect. This is a place transition. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Because the gate was locked, we waited.#Incorrect. This is a cause transition. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. What I noticed first was the empty chair at the end of the table.#Correct. This transition shifts the reader's attention to the detail the narrator notices first.
}

::CW_U02_L01_Q15::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which explanation best matches the revision? Draft: "I dropped the cup. Everyone stared." Revision: "When the cup hit the tile, the room went silent. A ring of coffee spread around my shoes while every face turned toward me."{
=A. The revision uses a time transition and description to make the moment more vivid.#Correct. "When" connects the moment in time, and the coffee and faces create a clear image.
~B. The revision removes the event sequence.#Incorrect. The sequence remains clear. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. The revision is stronger because it adds random details unrelated to the moment.#Incorrect. The details are related to the embarrassment and focus of the scene.
~D. The revision is stronger only because it is longer.#Incorrect. Length alone does not make writing stronger.
}

::CW_U02_L01_Q16::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which sentence uses reflection?{
~A. The elevator doors opened on the sixth floor.#Incorrect. This is action and setting movement. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. "Are you coming?" Dad asked.#Incorrect. This is dialogue. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. I knew then that I had not come to apologize; I had come to say goodbye.#Correct. This sentence shows the narrator's realization.
~D. A red suitcase leaned against the wall.#Incorrect. This is description. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_L01_Q17::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which transition best shows place?{
~A. After the bell rang, Mia stayed seated.#Incorrect. This shows time. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. At the far end of the hallway, Mia spotted the blue locker.#Correct. This transition places the action in a specific location.
~C. Because the hallway was crowded, Mia waited.#Incorrect. This shows cause. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Even though she was late, Mia smiled.#Incorrect. This shows contrast. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_L01_Q18::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] What should a writer do before choosing narrative techniques for a scene?{
~A. Add every technique so the scene includes everything.#Incorrect. Adding every technique can make a scene crowded and unfocused.
~B. Remove all transitions so the scene feels fast.#Incorrect. Fast pacing can still need transitions. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Start with the longest sentence possible.#Incorrect. Sentence length is a craft choice, but it should follow purpose.
=D. Decide what the scene needs the reader to experience or understand.#Correct. Purpose guides whether action, dialogue, description, or reflection is strongest.
}

::CW_U02_L01_Q19::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which choice best revises "The room was scary" using description?{
=A. The only light came from the hallway, and the closet door clicked softly in the dark.#Correct. Specific sensory details create a scary mood without directly naming it.
~B. The room was very, very scary.#Incorrect. Repeating "very" does not create a vivid scene.
~C. I was scared because the room was scary.#Incorrect. This tells the feeling without showing details.
~D. The room was a room in a building.#Incorrect. This gives no useful description. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_L01_Q20::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which statement about transitions is accurate?{
~A. Only the words first, next, and finally count as transitions.#Incorrect. Many words, phrases, and sentences can work as transitions.
~B. Transitions should never appear in creative writing.#Incorrect. Creative writing needs clear movement, too. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Transitions can show time, place, cause, contrast, sequence, or focus.#Correct. Narrative transitions guide the reader through the movement of the story.
~D. Transitions are only useful at the beginning of a paragraph.#Incorrect. Transitions can appear within sentences, between sentences, or between paragraphs.
}

::CW_U02_L01_Q21::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which sentence best uses dialogue to reveal conflict?{
~A. "Hello," I said.#Incorrect. This is dialogue, but it does not reveal clear conflict.
~B. "The weather is nice," she said.#Incorrect. This dialogue does not create conflict in this context.
~C. "I brought my notebook," he said.#Incorrect. This gives information but does not show conflict.
=D. "You promised you would not tell her," I said.#Correct. This line reveals a problem between characters.
}

::CW_U02_L01_Q22::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which choice best identifies the transition in this sentence? "Although the hallway was crowded, I could still hear my name from the office."{
~A. Time#Incorrect. The transition does not tell when something happens.
=B. Contrast#Correct. "Although" shows contrast between the crowded hallway and the narrator still hearing their name.
~C. Place#Incorrect. The sentence includes places, but "Although" is not a place transition.
~D. Cause#Incorrect. "Although" does not show cause; it shows contrast.
}

::CW_U02_L01_Q23::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which draft is most likely too much telling and not enough scene?{
=A. I was embarrassed, worried, and upset about the mistake.#Correct. This names feelings but does not show the moment through action, dialogue, description, or reflection.
~B. My pencil rolled off the desk and tapped against the silent floor.#Incorrect. This uses action and sound detail to create a scene.
~C. "Please do not read that out loud," I whispered.#Incorrect. This uses dialogue to show tension. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Around me, every chair scraped back at once.#Incorrect. This uses sound and action to create a scene.
}

::CW_U02_L01_Q24::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which choice gives the most useful peer feedback?{
~A. It is good.#Incorrect. This is too vague to guide revision.
=B. The scene would be clearer if you added a cause transition showing why the character leaves the room.#Correct. This feedback names a specific craft need and gives a revision direction.
~C. Add more stuff.#Incorrect. This does not identify what kind of craft move is needed.
~D. I do not like the character.#Incorrect. This is a personal reaction without a useful revision direction.
}

::CW_U02_L01_Q25::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which sentence best combines action and reflection?{
~A. The gate was silver.#Incorrect. This is description only. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. "Lock the gate," she said.#Incorrect. This is dialogue only. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. I locked the gate behind me and wondered whether leaving had made me brave or cruel.#Correct. The sentence shows an action and the narrator's thought about its meaning.
~D. I locked the gate.#Incorrect. This is action only and does not include reflection.
}
